
  Print the path of a worktree, named by its branch, its path or part of its
  branch, alone on stdout so that a shell can enter it, --quiet or not. `wtm go`
  is the shell function (`wtm shell-init`) that runs `wtm resolve` and enters the
  path it prints.

  On a terminal, a part of a branch several worktrees hold opens the picker over
  them, and no [worktree] the picker over every worktree, the cursor on the one
  you are in. Anywhere else (no terminal, --output json, --quiet) an ambiguous
  name is refused and no [worktree] resolves the one you are in. In text, nothing
  is printed on stdout unless a worktree is resolved.

  Usage:
    wtm-next resolve [worktree] [flags]

  Examples:
    wtm resolve feat/login

    # Use it in a script
    cd "$(wtm resolve feat/login)"

    wtm resolve login --output json

  Flags:
    -h, --help            help for resolve
        --output string   Output format: text or json (default "text")

  Global Flags:
    -q, --quiet   Silence human output; errors and the exit code are unaffected, and --output json still emits its document

